@meta
id: 08-14
title: Copy a block, put it above
chapter: 08
type: lesson
skills: yank-y, put-p, change-c, replace-r
par: 30
minutes: 5
vscode: select four lines, copy, click above, paste, fix the copy
open: config.ts

@brief
Counts work on yank as they do on everything else, and `P` puts the
register in above the cursor line rather than below it. Those two facts
turn "duplicate this block and change two things" into a handful of keys.

Staging has no entry in the gateway config, so it is falling through to
production's. Add one. It takes the same host as production, but only one
retry, and by convention environments are listed with staging first.

The `production` block stays exactly as it is.

@keys
| `4yy` | yank four lines |
| `P` | put the register above the cursor line |
| `p` | put it below |
| `cw` | change to the end of the word |
| `r` | replace one character |

@hints
The staging block is the production block with two values changed. Copy
four lines, put them where they belong, then correct them.
--
`4yy` yanks the current line and the three below. `P` is the one that
puts above — think of it as "put before", the opposite of `p`.
--
`j` `4yy` `P` gives you the copy in the right place with the cursor on
its first line. Then `^` `cw` `staging` `Esc`, and the retries line is
two down.

@solution
    j 4yy P  ^cwstaging<Esc>  2j $h r1  :wq<Enter>

Twenty-two keystrokes for a four-line block, and the values you did not
change cannot have drifted, because they were copied rather than retyped.

`p` and `P` are a pair you will use for the rest of your life, so get the
mnemonic straight early: lower case puts after the cursor, upper case
puts before it. For a linewise register that means below and above; for
anything else it means to the right and to the left of the cursor. The
next lessons are about what "anything else" means.

One habit to build: yank, then move, then put — in that order, and with
nothing in between. Any delete you sneak in while carrying something in
the register overwrites it, and `x` counts.

@start config.ts
export const gateway = {
  production: {
    host: 'pay.internal',
    retries: 3,
  },
};

@expected config.ts
export const gateway = {
  staging: {
    host: 'pay.internal',
    retries: 1,
  },
  production: {
    host: 'pay.internal',
    retries: 3,
  },
};
